{"kind":"artifact","text":"Two closed-environment claims carry their qualification where they are made, on a commit whose provenance is valid. Exact clean pushed head b2cee3752d120f5c6b292b286fb899a6d21c49f2 on request/repo-remote-link; tree f0d7072c, checkout clean, origin matching. Base: this branch's merge-base with main is c679fb73; main itself is 3a5d952c, both verified at filing. Under planner's recovery child ddaf16c9 and my promise 0e00bcd7.\n\nWHY THIS COMMIT EXISTS RATHER THAN THE LAST ONE. The identical textual change was committed at ebc0502a with a canonical Rests-On naming 1c12a6d3, a child that had been retired. I had predicted that resting on that row would poison the durable records, asked planner to re-anchor, and then built the commit against it while waiting for the answer. The promise and the artifacts moved to the fresh anchor when it arrived; a commit trailer cannot move. Codex declined the review on exactly that ground and was right to.\n\nTHE GENERAL FORM, because it is not specific to this lane. When a basis is judged unfit to rest on, that judgement covers the commit trailer as well as the durable filings, and covers it more strictly: every other record can be superseded, the trailer is immutable from the moment the commit exists. I had been treating what I build and what I file as separate questions with separate anchors. They are one question. I also had the chance to catch it at push time and did not — I verified head, tree, branch, cleanliness and origin equality, and not the single field the whole exchange had been about.\n\nWHAT THE CHANGE IS. gitStatedEnvironment at app.go:214 and the closed-world test at repo_remote_test.go:711 both opened absolutely and narrowed to Unix several paragraphs later; standing alone each was false, because Go's os/exec adds SYSTEMROOT to what a Windows child receives. Both now carry the qualification in the opening sentence. Line numbers re-derived at this head rather than carried from the withdrawn commit. The tree is byte-identical to ebc0502a, so nothing about the code differs — only the provenance does.\n\nTHE SWEEP. Round eight asked for the rest of internal/app and its tests rather than only the two lines codex named, since a reviewer names what they opened. No instance beyond those two.\n\nTHE PROVENANCE VALUES ABOVE ARE RE-DERIVED AT FILING. The earlier sets said \"base equals current main c679fb73\", carried forward through four republications after it stopped being true. The head, tree and branch are pinned by the commit and survive being carried; the base is a relationship between two moving things and has to be retaken each time.\n\nNO BEHAVIOUR CHANGES. The construction, the regressions, their hard controls and the citation exit-code fail-closed rule are exactly as reviewed at 5a4ee9e9.\n\nWHAT THIS ARTIFACT DOES NOT CLAIM. It does not assert that the operator's ambient environment is hostile. Planner relays at c74757f5 that hugh has reconsidered and intends that environment to be outside gitseq's threat model. What the code does is stated here; whether the project wants it for that reason is a question the clarified ruling may reframe.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, GOOS=windows go build ./internal/app/ and git diff --check all exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux.","body":{"commit":"b2cee3752d120f5c6b292b286fb899a6d21c49f2","path":"internal/app"}}